### Summary

`RegistryClient::flat_single_index` in `registry_client.rs` (line ~465) takes the async mutex guard before checking the cache, then holds it across the network fetch:

```rust
let mut cache = self.flat_indexes.lock().await;
if let Some(entries) = cache.get(index) {
    return Ok(/* ... */);
}
// still holding cache lock
let FlatIndexEntries { entries, .. } =
    client.fetch_index(index).await.map_err(ErrorKind::Flat)?;
```

If two packages need entries from different flat indexes, the second one blocks on the mutex until the first finishes its HTTP request. With slow or many flat indexes this serializes what could otherwise be concurrent fetches.

A fetch-then-insert pattern or per-index in-flight dedup would let unrelated index requests proceed without blocking each other.

